{
  "name": "Hourly generator check-out calls to BPA",
  "today": "Every hour, around the clock, the trader on shift at the Portland real-time desk calls the service-customer generator (Frontier, and until it moves to BPA's automatic system, Grays Harbor) for its actual output, then calls BPA to read the actual figure in, then initials the hour on the services sheet. This must be done even when the generator is prescheduled. In June the hourly BPA check-out also covered GCPUD. The calls compete with trading and were often missed. BPA complained that service was inconsistent and threatened to report that Enron could not provide the service.",
  "change": "Meter the remaining service generators automatically so hourly actuals reach BPA and the services sheet without a trader's calls.",
  "explanation": "Have the remaining service generators (Frontier, and GCPUD if it is still covered) automatically metered or telemetered to BPA. BPA said it would set up the same kind of automatic recording for Grays Harbor. Bill Williams's instruction already treats automatic metering as the point where the hourly calls end. After the change the real-time traders stop the hourly generator and BPA calls and the initialling. They step in only when the meter feed fails. Grays Harbor is left out of the savings because BPA was already automating it and it stopped being a customer on Oct. 1.",
  "occurrences": [
    {
      "file": "corpus/williams-w3/sent_items/357.eml",
      "quote": "We must call Grays Harbor and Frontier at the top of EVERY hour.  Then we need to call BPA and communicate the ACTUAL energy generated, not an estimate.  Finally, sign off the hour in the services book."
    },
    {
      "file": "corpus/williams-w3/sent_items/506.eml",
      "quote": "We must check out with BPA each hour for Frontier and GCPUD.  Please make the call right after you call EPE dispatchers (between 25 after and 35 after)."
    }
  ],
  "evidence": [
    {
      "file": "corpus/williams-w3/sent_items/357.eml",
      "quote": "If this process takes more than 5 minutes, please let me know.",
      "note": "The whole hourly process, then covering two generators plus BPA, is expected to take under 5 minutes. With only Frontier left, about 3 minutes is a realistic figure."
    },
    {
      "file": "corpus/williams-w3/sent_items/357.eml",
      "quote": "we need to do this even if the generator is prescheduled, and must to continue to do this until these generators are automatically metered.",
      "note": "Automatic metering ends the calls. They happen even when the output is already known from the preschedule."
    },
    {
      "file": "corpus/williams-w3/sent_items/357.eml",
      "quote": "Warren is inclined to recommend to BPA that we are incapable of this service because of our inconsistent performance.",
      "note": "The calls were being missed. That put the services business at risk."
    },
    {
      "file": "corpus/williams-w3/sent_items/335.eml",
      "quote": "BPA is saying they'll have a automatic system in place to record these numbers automatically by Mid August so hang in there.",
      "note": "BPA was setting up automatic recording for Grays Harbor, so this kind of automation is feasible. Grays is excluded from the savings."
    },
    {
      "file": "corpus/williams-w3/sent_items/335.eml",
      "quote": "on Oct. 1 Grays will no longer be our customer",
      "note": "Grays Harbor leaves, so only the other generators count."
    }
  ],
  "ongoing": true,
  "stated_rate": {
    "count": 24,
    "per": "day",
    "file": "corpus/williams-w3/sent_items/357.eml",
    "quote": "at the top of EVERY hour"
  },
  "minutes": 3
}

{
  "name": "Gas flowed at a meter after the deal expired, or with no deal",
  "change": "Roll expired deals forward automatically, write off small volumes under a standing approval, and send the remaining items to the trader as one electronic batch list.",
  "reason": "The 20 items a month mostly come from Clem Cernosek's memos, a one-time burst of 1999-2000 flows sent from December 2000 to January 2001, and that same work has already been rejected; also, the memos are not small (2002.eml has 11,980 MMBtu, 1985.eml 2,164), rolling a deal forward 'at the same terms' goes against the trader's word that such deals must be renegotiated (1458.eml), and 804.eml does not show a six-month stall on an expired deal.",
  "research": "Look for ongoing expired-deal or no-deal requests after January 2001 (Lannou, Young, Olsen, McCoy, Poorman and others) to show a steady monthly rate, and for traders extending deals unchanged, to show how many could safely roll forward; a proposal limited to batching the existing feedback-monitor list for one approval might hold up on that evidence."
}

{
  "name": "Counterparty MTM valuations for auditors",
  "today": "Counterparties' auditors need third-party mark-to-market (MTM) values of their deals with Enron as of a past date, usually year-end. Confirmations (Joe Hunter, Ellen Wallumrod) must supply them but has no system that values deals. So each request is emailed to five or six gas desk heads at once. Each head forwards it to someone on the desk (Giron sends West desk requests to Pat Ryder), who runs a back-dated mini-book, exports it to Excel and emails it back. The mini-book also carries Delta/Vol figures that must be blacked out before it goes to the counterparty. Every request pulls Risk staff off their own book work across several desks, and the answers have to be collected from each one.",
  "change": "Give Confirmations a read-only valuation view with back-dated curves so it can produce these valuations itself, without going to the risk desks.",
  "explanation": "Build the read-only 'EnTelligence' view that Confirmations itself asked for (or an equivalent report): it keeps daily price curves, so it can value all of a counterparty's deals as of any past date, and it leaves out the Delta/Vol columns. Confirmations enters the counterparty short name and the date, runs the report and sends it to the auditor. Desk heads and desk risk staff are no longer involved, and nothing has to be blacked out.",
  "occurrences": [
    {
      "file": "corpus/giron-d/sent/240.eml",
      "quote": "Please forward to Ellen Wallumrod the MTM valuation(mini-book) for all \nCoEnergy Trading Company deals as of Dec 31, 2000.  If you can export into \nexcel and forward by e-mail, that would be appreciated."
    },
    {
      "file": "corpus/giron-d/sent/214.eml",
      "quote": "Can you provide MTM values as of Dec 29, 2000, for the short name of CROSS \nTIMBERS."
    }
  ],
  "evidence": [
    {
      "file": "corpus/giron-d/sent/241.eml",
      "quote": "Until we can get EnTelligence built to our specifications, \nwe'll need to continue to go to the different desks to provide Mark-to-Market \nvaluations for a Counterparty's portfolio.",
      "note": "Each request goes to several desks because Confirmations cannot value deals itself."
    },
    {
      "file": "corpus/giron-d/sent/241.eml",
      "quote": "We do not have access to \nany system that values these deals, but are looking to get the on-line \n\"EnTelligence\" built so that it carries back-dated curves that will allow us \nto complete these requests without taking up a Risk person's time and without \nhaving to black out information (Delta/Vol) that is included in a mini-book.",
      "note": "Confirmations names the cause (no valuation access), the cost (Risk staff time and manual blacking out of Delta/Vol) and the fix."
    },
    {
      "file": "corpus/giron-d/sent/241.eml",
      "quote": "Call Pat Ryder for West desk requests.",
      "note": "Within a desk the head passes the work on to a second person, so each request touches two people per desk."
    }
  ],
  "ongoing": true,
  "stated_rate": {
    "count": 3,
    "per": "week",
    "file": "corpus/giron-d/sent/241.eml",
    "quote": "we recieve several \nper week"
  },
  "minutes": 42
}

