{
  "name": "Daily TAGG 'Rebook to' corrections on the Credit Report",
  "change": "Keep a Credit alias table in TAGG/Global Counterparty that substitutes or blocks recurring wrong counterparty names at deal entry.",
  "reason": "The only evidence of how often rebooks happen is the same Giron Credit Report forwards already saved as 'Daily hand-forwarding of the Credit Report', the separate rebook requests in the mail are only a handful, and the proposal overstates its case: the names recur weeks apart, not daily; about 40% of rebook lines repeat, not half; and automatic substitution would misbook, because the right entity for the same name changes.",
  "research": "Look in the book administrators' and Credit's own mailboxes (Carole Frank, Derek Bailey, Julie Brewer, Lesli Campbell, Russell Diamond) for rebook requests and 'Done' replies apart from Giron's Credit Report forwards, or for a message that says how many deals are rebooked each day or week; that would give the rebook work its own occurrences and rate, so a warning-only version of the change could be judged on its own."
}

{
  "name": "Nightly counterparty checkouts in Enpower Path Confirmation, Portland Real Time desk",
  "change": "Pre-fill counterparty contact details and make the Path Confirmation save run in the background, one batch per counterparty.",
  "reason": "The only evidence is one memo launching the tool (86.eml, 19 Nov 2001), sent two weeks before Enron's bankruptcy while counterparties were already stopping trades with Enron; nothing shows 25 counterparties being confirmed every night for long, the one minute of contact typing and the retyping of the same contacts are made up, and this same work and change were already rejected in decisions.md.",
  "research": "Find messages from December 2001 onward (Enron estate or UBS Warburg Energy) showing the Real Time desk still confirming many counterparties' paths in Enpower Path Confirmation each night, with any note on save time or contact entry."
}

{
  "name": "Transwestern short-term contract-quantity changes",
  "change": "Shippers submit one-day or few-day MDQ increases and month-to-month continuations on a web form; the marketer approves with one click and the contract system generates the amendment.",
  "reason": "The rate is not supported: all six distinct requests fall in 10-31 Dec 2001, and Lokay's sent mail from January to late March 2002 (about 100 messages) has none, so 6 a month is a one-month burst projected forward. The 15-minute amendment step is also assumed, and the marketer mostly forwards a one-word 'Approved.' rather than retyping terms.",
  "research": "Find short-term MDQ or continuation requests outside December 2001 in other TW marketers' or Contract Administration's mail (Dennis Lee, Michael Bodnar, Lindberg, Lohman, Y'Barbo, McConnell), and anything showing how long Contract Admin spends on a one-day amendment."
}

{
  "name": "Month-end basis and index roll-off in the gas risk books",
  "today": "At each prompt-month roll-off the gas risk lead, Jeff Gossett, emails the book administrators the steps to follow. On each roll-off night every administrator runs a basis calc twice: first with 100% of basis in the prompt month, which is officialized and loaded into TDS, then the book is unofficialized and calculated again with 50% of basis rolled off, and officialized for the Benchmark used for V@R. Gossett suggests using two post IDs. Half of the Gas Daily and physical index positions roll off and roll in each night, over two nights. The work depends on the email and on each administrator remembering the sequence. In November some traders' basis was not rolled off on the Benchmark, and Giron said it was because he forgot to remind Kevin Radous, so V@R was wrong.",
  "change": "Make the roll-off a scheduled setting in the risk calc, so one run produces both the 100% TDS view and the rolled V@R view.",
  "explanation": "Put the roll-off dates and percentages for basis and for Gas Daily and physical index into the calc as a calendar setting kept by the risk group. On roll-off nights a single calc writes the 100% prompt-month basis to the TDS post ID and the rolled-off basis to the Benchmark/V@R post ID, so administrators calculate and officialize once, as on any other night. A simple check lists any book whose Benchmark basis was not rolled, for Gossett or the regional lead to follow up. The monthly instruction email and the second calc, with its unofficialize and re-officialize steps, go away.",
  "occurrences": [
    {
      "file": "corpus/giron-d/sent/631.eml",
      "quote": "Again this month, you are going to have to calculate your basis positions \n(officialize) twice.  Once for the TDS download, and once for the Benchmark \nfor V@R."
    },
    {
      "file": "corpus/giron-d/sent/442.eml",
      "quote": "On the Benchmark, these traders basis positions were not rolled off."
    }
  ],
  "evidence": [
    {
      "file": "corpus/giron-d/sent/631.eml",
      "quote": "Just to be clear,  calc your 100% basis calc first.  Officialize.  Load your \npositions into TDS. Unofficialize. Calc your 50% basis calc.  Officialize.",
      "note": "The hand sequence each administrator repeats on each roll-off night. The second calc and the official/unofficial toggling are the steps the change removes."
    },
    {
      "file": "corpus/giron-d/sent/631.eml",
      "quote": "For the V@R calculations we will need to roll 50% of basis off \ntonight,  and 50% tommorrow.",
      "note": "The extra work runs over two nights each month."
    },
    {
      "file": "corpus/giron-d/sent/631.eml",
      "quote": "Also,  we will roll-off 50% of all index positions (Gas Daily and Physical) \ntonight and roll-in 50% of physical and gas daily index.  And the other 50% \ntommorrow.",
      "note": "The index roll-off is also split over two nights, which the schedule setting would handle."
    },
    {
      "file": "corpus/giron-d/sent/442.eml",
      "quote": "Is \nthere something further that I need to do to make sure that happens in the \nfuture?",
      "note": "Where it stalls: the roll-off was missed for several traders, and the risk lead asks how to stop it happening again."
    },
    {
      "file": "corpus/giron-d/sent/442.eml",
      "quote": "My fault on Tomas, I didn't remind Kevin.  It won't happen again.",
      "note": "The roll-off depends on people reminding each other."
    }
  ],
  "ongoing": true,
  "stated_rate": {
    "count": 1,
    "per": "month",
    "file": "corpus/giron-d/sent/631.eml",
    "quote": "Again this month"
  },
  "minutes": 330
}

{
  "name": "Setting up new forward curves in RisktRAC and TAGG",
  "today": "A desk risk manager builds a new curve in ERMS. To get it into the V@R system (RisktRAC) the risk manager fills in a paper curve set-up request form and sends it to Risk Analysis and Reporting (RAR), who key it into RisktRAC. Separately, the risk manager emails Jeremy Wong to add the curve to TAGG, and waits for his reply. Steps get skipped: RAR found 'numerous curves in ERMS that are not in RisktRAC', which leaves positions out of V@R. RAR now checks ERMS against RisktRAC by hand every week to catch the gaps.",
  "change": "Set up each new ERMS curve in RisktRAC and TAGG automatically and report any gaps nightly, instead of request forms, emails and a weekly manual check.",
  "explanation": "Treat ERMS as the master list of curves. When a risk manager creates a curve in ERMS, a job creates the matching curve in RisktRAC and in TAGG, or puts it in a queue for RAR and the TAGG administrator to approve with one click. A nightly report lists any ERMS curve still missing from RisktRAC or TAGG. Afterwards the risk manager creates the curve once and sends no form or email; RAR and the TAGG administrator only handle the items on the exception report, and RAR no longer runs its weekly comparison by hand.",
